    "case_id": "532",
    "case_summary": "A 66-year-old female with a history of epilepsy, thyroid cancer status post thyroidectomy and radiation, hypertension, hyperlipidemia, and obstructive sleep apnea with nightly continuous positive airway pressure use. She presented with progressively worsening dyspnea on exertion over 9 months. Initially, she could walk over 1 mile on flat land without stopping, but the condition progressed to requiring multiple breaks during household activities and needing a walker for short distances. No associated lower extremity edema, orthopnea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, chest pain, palpitations, dizziness, or lightheadedness. She is a lifetime nonsmoker.\n\nExercise testing showed rapid dyspnea within 45 seconds of initiation. Significant decreases in systolic and diastolic blood pressure (average -20 mm Hg and -12 mm Hg, respectively) immediately after exercise on two separate attempts.\n\n- Transthoracic echocardiogram (TTE):  - Normal left ventricular ejection fraction of 60% to 65%  - Lipomatous hypertrophy of the interatrial septum present  - Pulmonary function tests:  - Normal results  - Chest CT:  - Image finding: 3.1 × 2.3 × 3.0 cm well-defined mass within the lower SVC with extension to the interatrial septum  - Cardiac MRI and MRA findings:  - Lentiform fat signal mass between lateral wall of ascending thoracic aorta and medial wall of SVC measuring 2.8 cm × 1.5 cm  - SVC peak flow velocity: 74 cm/s cephalad to mass, 124 cm/s caudal to mass  - MRA showed narrowing of the SVC and decreased blood flow through the site of compression  Images referenced:- Figure 1: CT chest (coronal view)- Figure 2: TTE 4-chamber view- Figure 3: Thoracic MRI coronal plane- Figure 4: MRA TWIST sequence of thoracic vessels",
